A||||7||Fiero_24||Thermostat problems||||5||
Z||000000||Fiero_24||11-16-2001||10:06 PM||||Here is a pic of 3 thermostat's.  The left side is a new thermostat, the middle one is what has happened to 3 of them in a row, and the one on the right came in the car.  Does anyone know what would bend a thermostat like that?  Has anyone ever seen a thermostat like the one on the right?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Thermostat.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-16-2001||10:43 PM||||You sure you're seating them all the way before you put the cap on? Put some vasilene on the "O" ring and make sure it snaps down into position when installing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rodmcneill||11-17-2001||01:59 AM||||Check for rust inside the neck that would prevent the thermostat from going all the way down and seating. If you find rust inside the neck just use a little emery cloth to remove any rust ridge.||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||11-17-2001||10:50 AM||||I don't see how a thermostat could be anywhere near seated and get bent like the middle one. The top of the 'bail should be at least flush with where the cap ends up. Question! Is it possible for pressure to build up under the thermostat and force it up against the cap, bending the bail in the process?||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||11-17-2001||11:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>I don't see how a thermostat could be anywhere near seated and get bent like the middle one. The top of the 'bail should be at least flush with where the cap ends up. Question! Is it possible for pressure to build up under the thermostat and force it up against the cap, bending the bail in the process?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The last sentence is <B>EXACTLY</B> what is happening!!!  I have had 3 examples of the above middle failures.  Straightening up the "legs" will not help as the pressure from the water pump @ high rpms (above 4000) just pushes the thermostat back into a pretzel!<P>My housing is devoid of rust, the thermostat installs without a problem (slight pressure to get the rubber gasket on the thermostat to seat), the cap fits on top of the "handle" to keep it in place (if the cap will not engage then the thermostat isn't install down far enough) and with one "buzz" of the engine, the thermostat collapse!<P>Currently, the latest, brand new thermostat is holding together.  (fingers crossed)<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_24||11-17-2001||01:34 PM||||I thought pressure was the problem, but why?  I have my new motor going in, so I guess I will have to see if it still happens.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||11-17-2001||02:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_24:<BR><B>I thought pressure was the problem, but why?  I have my new motor going in, so I guess I will have to see if it still happens.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I would guess the problem is air or gas building up under the thermostat. Perhaps you had a leaky headgasket.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ka4nkf||11-17-2001||11:58 PM||||The only other thing I can think of is the radiator cap maybe it is bad and if it gets hot and the cap don't open then you would have a lot of pressure in the system.||||reg||1
